menu "HID bridge (application configuration)"

    config APP_ENABLE_HID_HOST
        bool "Central role: receive reports from a BLE keyboard and mouse"
        depends on !APP_ROLE_FAKE_KEYBOARD
        default y
        help
            Enables the BLE scanner and esp_hidh (GATT client). Can be turned off to
            test the pad side alone, without scanning competing for antenna time.

    config APP_ENABLE_GAMEPAD
        bool "Peripheral role: present a HID gamepad to the PC"
        depends on !APP_ROLE_FAKE_KEYBOARD
        default y
        help
            Enables our own GATT server with HID service 0x1812 and advertising.

    choice APP_GAMEPAD_PROFILE
        prompt "Pad: profile the PC sees"
        depends on APP_ENABLE_GAMEPAD
        default APP_GAMEPAD_PROFILE_XBOX
        help
            Decides what the pad claims to be.

            NOTE: switching the profile changes the report descriptor AND the device
            identity, so it requires removing the pad from the Windows Bluetooth
            device list and pairing again (AGENTS.md 4.7).

        config APP_GAMEPAD_PROFILE_GENERIC
            bool "Generic HID pad (DirectInput, visible in joy.cpl)"
            help
                4 signed 8-bit axes + 12 buttons, with our own VID/PID.
                Games that only speak XInput will NOT see this pad.
                This is the profile verified first - kept as a fallback.

        config APP_GAMEPAD_PROFILE_XBOX
            bool "Xbox Series X pad (XInput)"
            help
                The bridge impersonates a wireless Xbox Series X controller (model
                1914): the report descriptor is byte-for-byte that of a real pad, and
                the PnP ID carries Microsoft's VID with PID 0x0B13. Windows then loads
                its own driver and exposes the device through XInput.

                The PID is not arbitrary - xinputhid.inf binds only to 0x0B13 and
                0x0B20..0x0B27, see AGENTS.md 4.32.

                Axes are 16-bit, triggers 10-bit, and there is a hat switch plus 15
                buttons. Input mapping details: AGENTS.md 4.31.
    endchoice

    config APP_GAMEPAD_SELFTEST
        bool "Pad: synthetic test pattern instead of real inputs"
        depends on APP_ENABLE_GAMEPAD
        default n
        help
            Instead of mapping the keyboard and mouse, the pad sweeps its left stick
            around a circle and presses buttons in turn. Lets you verify in joy.cpl
            that the report descriptor is correct without connecting any input device.

    config APP_GAMEPAD_DEVICE_NAME
        string "Pad: name advertised over BLE"
        depends on APP_ENABLE_GAMEPAD
        default "Xbox Wireless Controller" if APP_GAMEPAD_PROFILE_XBOX
        default "C3 Gamepad"
        help
            The name shown in the Windows Bluetooth device list. Changing the name does
            not require re-pairing, unlike changing the report descriptor.

            In the Xbox profile it defaults to what a real pad uses - Windows shows it
            in the Bluetooth settings, and when impersonating a device there is no
            reason for it to differ.

    config APP_PAD_CONN_ITVL
        int "Pad: target connection interval to the PC [units of 1.25 ms]"
        depends on APP_ENABLE_GAMEPAD
        range 6 40
        default 6
        help
            Interval of the pad -> PC link. This is the NARROWEST POINT of the whole
            chain: the mouse can report as often as it likes, but only what fits through
            this link reaches the game.

            We do not set this interval ourselves - Windows is the central on this link
            and it picks the value. A peripheral can only ASK for a change, through the
            LL Connection Parameters Request procedure or L2CAP. Windows may refuse.

            The default of 6 (7.5 ms = 133 Hz) follows from a real Xbox pad doing 125 Hz
            over BT - so Windows is clearly able to hold such a link. The firmware walks
            a ladder from this value and logs what it managed to get.

    config APP_REPORT_RATE_HZ
        int "Pad: report task frequency [Hz]"
        depends on APP_ENABLE_GAMEPAD
        range 10 250
        default 100
        help
            The task wakes up at this rate, but a report is only sent to the PC when the
            pad state has actually changed.

    config APP_INPUT_CONN_ITVL
        int "Inputs: target connection interval [units of 1.25 ms]"
        depends on APP_ENABLE_HID_HOST
        range 6 40
        default 12
        help
            Upper limit on the report rate from the keyboard and mouse. A peripheral can
            only send a notification during a connection event, so the interval decides
            everything - even a mouse with a high polling rate cannot send more.

            Value in units of 1.25 ms:
              6  = 7.5 ms  -> 133 Hz  (minimum allowed by the BLE spec)
              8  = 10 ms   -> 100 Hz
              12 = 15 ms   ->  66 Hz  (default; also what Windows negotiates for the pad)
              24 = 30 ms   ->  33 Hz
              36 = 45 ms   ->  22 Hz  (what we got before this was implemented)

            MEASURED ON HARDWARE: the C3 controller REJECTS intervals of 7.5 / 10 /
            12.5 ms with HCI error 0x12 and accepts 15 ms. Verified that neither ce_len
            nor the supervision timeout is the cause - the same values with those changed
            were rejected too. Details in AGENTS.md 4.33.

            Leaving a smaller value here does no harm: the firmware walks a ladder and
            uses the shortest interval the controller accepts. The log shows each attempt.

    config APP_MOUSE_SCALE_DIV
        int "Mapping: mouse sensitivity divisor"
        depends on APP_ENABLE_GAMEPAD && APP_ENABLE_HID_HOST
        range 1 128
        default 24
        help
            The larger the value, the less sensitive the mouse. Full axis deflection
            corresponds to an average delta of (this value * 4) mouse counts per task
            tick, i.e. 96 counts per tick at the default of 24.

            Where that number comes from: measurements showed up to ~355 counts per
            report during brisk movement, and reports arrive every ~45 ms, i.e. ~79
            counts per tick. With the previous default of 8 (full deflection at 32
            counts) the stick hit maximum almost immediately. At 24 the same movement
            gives around 80 % deflection, while careful aiming stays in the lower part
            of the range.

            If it is still too fast, raise it to 32 or 48. The change only requires a
            rebuild - re-pairing the pad is NOT needed, because the descriptor does not
            change.

    config APP_ROLE_FAKE_KEYBOARD
        bool "Diagnostics: act as a synthetic BLE keyboard instead of a bridge"
        default n
        help
            Turns the board into an advertiser that impersonates the AULA F99 Pro in pairing
            mode, byte for byte, and accepts connections. Nothing else runs - no HID host, no
            gamepad.

            What it is for: that keyboard pairs with the bridge on the C3 and S3 and never on
            the C6 or H2, and the difference was narrowed to the controller library, which is
            a prebuilt blob (AGENTS.md 4.35). This role replaces a device we cannot change
            with one we can, so the properties of the advertiser - interval, address type,
            discoverability flags - can be bisected against a failing initiator.

            Run it on a second board, with the real keyboard switched off so it cannot
            interfere.

    config APP_FAKE_KBD_ADV_ITVL_MS
        int "Synthetic keyboard: advertising interval in milliseconds"
        depends on APP_ROLE_FAKE_KEYBOARD
        range 20 10240
        default 200
        help
            The measured original advertises every 50-900 ms, so 200 ms sits in the middle of
            its range. Raise it to test whether a sparse advertiser is what the initiator
            struggles with.

    config APP_FAKE_KBD_RANDOM_ADDR
        bool "Synthetic keyboard: advertise from a static random address"
        depends on APP_ROLE_FAKE_KEYBOARD
        default y
        help
            The original uses a static random address (type 1). Turn this off to advertise
            from the public address instead - that is one of the bisection steps, because the
            initiator's address filtering is a suspect.

    config APP_FAKE_KBD_FLAGS
        hex "Synthetic keyboard: the AD flags byte"
        depends on APP_ROLE_FAKE_KEYBOARD
        default 0x05
        help
            0x05 is what the original sends: LE Limited Discoverable plus BR/EDR not
            supported. 0x06 swaps Limited for General Discoverable, which is what most
            devices use - worth trying, because Limited mode is the unusual choice here.

    config APP_FAKE_KBD_TX_POWER
        int "Synthetic keyboard: transmit power level index (15 = +20 dBm, 0 = -24 dBm)"
        depends on APP_ROLE_FAKE_KEYBOARD
        range 0 15
        default 15
        help
            Lets the synthetic keyboard be made quiet on purpose, which is the only way we
            have to simulate distance without moving hardware.

            Why it matters: the first run of this role connected on the H2 in 560 ms - but at
            rssi -7 dBm, because both boards sit on the same PCB. The real keyboard fails on
            the H2 at -40 dBm and succeeds on the S3 at -79 dBm. So the open question is
            whether the C6/H2 INITIATOR is deaf at distances where its SCANNER hears fine.
            Sweeping this value gives the rssi at which connecting stops working, which is a
            number, not a hypothesis.

            The index is esp_power_level_t: 15 = +20 dBm, 11 = +9 dBm, 8 = 0 dBm, 4 = -12 dBm,
            0 = -24 dBm.

    config APP_DEBUG_CTRL_LOG_DUMP
        bool "Diagnostics: dump the BLE controller's internal log after a failed open"
        default n
        help
            The C6/H2 controller keeps its own log, including the HCI traffic between host and
            controller, and can dump it on demand (esp_ble_controller_log_dump_all). With this
            option the bridge triggers that dump the moment a device open fails.

            Why: every conclusion about AGENTS.md 4.35 so far rests on "Connection failed;
            status=13", which is a HOST-side timeout and says nothing about what the
            controller did. The dump is the controller's own account of the same seconds.

            Needs the controller log compiled in, which is a separate set of options:
              CONFIG_BT_LE_CONTROLLER_LOG_ENABLED=y
              CONFIG_BT_LE_CONTROLLER_LOG_HCI_ENABLED=y     (default y)
              CONFIG_BT_LE_CONTROLLER_LOG_DUMP_ONLY=y       (default y, buffers in RAM)

            The intended use is a pair of runs on the SAME board: one dialling the real
            keyboard, which fails, and one dialling the synthetic keyboard from
            APP_ROLE_FAKE_KEYBOARD, which succeeds. The difference between the two dumps is
            the step where it goes wrong.

    config APP_DEBUG_SCAN_ONLY
        bool "Diagnostics: scan and log only, never connect to an input device"
        default n
        help
            The scanner normally stops as soon as it hears a usable candidate and hands the
            address to the connection path, which makes it impossible to measure what a
            device is actually doing in the air: how often it advertises, and how quickly it
            changes its address.

            With this option the bridge keeps scanning and only logs. Every advertising
            packet from a HID-looking device is printed with its address, RSSI, flags and raw
            payload, so a long run gives the advertising interval and the address rotation
            rate directly.

            Written to characterise the keyboard from AGENTS.md 4.35, whose advertising
            behaviour has to be reproduced by a synthetic advertiser before the difference
            between the C3/S3 and C6/H2 controllers can be bisected.

    config APP_DEBUG_WATCH_ADDR
        hex "Diagnostics: address guarded by a hardware watchpoint (0 = disabled)"
        default 0
        help
            When non-zero, the firmware arms a hardware write watchpoint on this address
            (4 bytes) at startup. On a write it panics with the backtrace of the CULPRIT
            rather than the victim - the only way to find out who is corrupting a static
            structure inside somebody else's component.

            Used to track down the crash in AGENTS.md 4.21. The address of the GAP
            connection-parameter update list head is read from the ELF file:

              riscv32-esp-elf-nm build.esp32c3/hid_gamepad_bridge.elf \\
                  | grep ble_gap_update_entries

            NOTE: the address changes between builds, so it must be re-read after every
            code change and checked for having moved.

endmenu
